<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thohanbwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thohanbwa (died 1543) was a Shan prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawlon">Sawlon of Mohnyin</a>, who became king of Ava after the Shan confederation captured the city in 1527. Distrusting the Burmese monkhood, he became notorious for killing monks and plundering pagodas. He was assassinated in 1543 by his own ministers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>'s Toungoo later broke Shan power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thohanbwa, Hso Harn Hpa in Shan, was born in 1505, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawlon">Sawlon of Mohnyin</a>, who had rebelled successfully against Ava in the 1480s. Father and son raided Ava's lands from the north, and in the early 1520s a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a> under Mohnyin joined them, stripping Ava of its northernmost territories and sacking the city in 1524. When the confederation besieged Ava again in 1527, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwenankyawshin">Shwenankyawshin</a> was killed in battle in April and the city fell. Sawlon made his son king of Ava, in effect a viceroy for himself and the confederation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Unused to governing so large a kingdom, the two raiders kept ministers of the old court and made Mingyi Yan Naung chief minister, while many of Ava's people fled south to Toungoo. In 1533 they attacked Prome, their former ally, and carried off its ruler Bayin Htwe, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawlon">Sawlon</a> was assassinated by his own ministers on the march home. Thohanbwa became chief of Mohnyin as well as king of Ava, yet the other saophas did not accept him as first among equals, and the confederation did nothing while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> of Toungoo made war on Hanthawaddy from 1534 to 1539.</p><p>Burmese history remembers him for his attacks on Buddhist pagodas and monks. He held that pagodas were treasure chambers with nothing to do with religion and plundered those within reach. In 1540 he had 360 monks of Ava, Sagaing and Pinya killed, 30 of them noted for their learning, and burned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the monasteries. The historian G. E. Harvey called him a full-blooded savage, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> and Shans alike hated him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Only when Toungoo turned on Prome, Ava's vassal, did Thohanbwa lead the confederation's states, Mohnyin, Mogaung, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a>, Momeik, Hsipaw and Yawnghwe, to its defence. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of Toungoo defeated them outside Prome. With his allies at court gone, Yan Naung staged a coup at the summer palace outside Ava: asked to bring the late king's sword, he bowed as if to present it and ran the king through. Yan Naung declined the throne, which passed to Hkonmaing, ruler of Hsipaw, a steadfast ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwenankyawshin">Shwenankyawshin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thohanbwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sawlon placed his son <b>Thohanbwa</b> on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/confederation-of-shan-states">Confederation of Shan States</a></li><li><blockquote>Sawlon's son <b>Thohanbwa</b> then reigned at Ava, notorious for attacking monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohnyin">Mohnyin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Inwa, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.86,95.99&amp;year=1533" aria-label="Inwa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7124 1356 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Thohanbwa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7574 1626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7574 1626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=21.86,95.99&amp;year=1533">What was here in 1533? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
